MyGo patch 0009 (VoxtMini): Updater.Configured.

An app may want to honour developer overrides (say, environment
variables naming other executables to run) only in development builds:
in a published one, a malicious process of the user could set them
(launchctl setenv) and have the app run its code, which then inherits
the app's privacy permissions. The one thing that tells a published
build from others is the update feed `mygo build` links in, and it was
unexported. Updater.Configured reports whether the build links an
update feed and its key; unlike Enabled it ignores where the app is
installed and MYGO_ENV. TestUpdaterConfigured.
diff --git a/updater.go b/updater.go
index 8965e5e..bd3bb4f 100644
--- a/updater.go
+++ b/updater.go
@@ -102,6 +102,16 @@ func (u *UpdaterModule) Enabled() bool {
 	return true
 }
 
+// Configured reports whether the build links an update feed and its key:
+// `mygo build` with updates in the configuration, which is how apps tell
+// their published builds from development ones (an app may honour
+// developer overrides, such as other executables to run, only in the
+// latter). Unlike Enabled it ignores where the app is installed and
+// MYGO_ENV.
+func (u *UpdaterModule) Configured() bool {
+	return packageUpdateFeed != "" && packageUpdateKey != ""
+}
+
 // Check asks the update feed for a version newer than the running one and
 // returns it, or nil when the app is up to date.
 func (u *UpdaterModule) Check(ctx context.Context) (*Update, error) {
